WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.211
Three computers share one link, plugged into a switch, the box that joins their cables.

2
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:11.851
Client wants to ping peer, at 10.10.1.20.

3
00:00:11.851 --> 00:00:15.648
A ping is a small test message: are you there?

4
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.578
Every network card has a name, its MAC address: six bytes, like this one.

5
00:00:20.578 --> 00:00:26.857
And every frame, each chunk of data sent across the link, must carry the MAC of the card it's for.

6
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.325
Client knows peer's IP address.

7
00:00:29.325 --> 00:00:30.402
But peer's MAC?

8
00:00:30.402 --> 00:00:31.837
Nothing has told it.

9
00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:33.847
So where does client get it?

10
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.033
The tempting answer is a list.

11
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:38.869
Surely something keeps every card's address.

12
00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:40.157
The switch, perhaps?

13
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.502
But a switch knows only MAC addresses, and which port each one sits behind.

14
00:00:45.502 --> 00:00:47.475
It never reads an IP address.

15
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.722
Then give every computer a file of its neighbours.

16
00:00:50.722 --> 00:00:53.260
It breaks the first time anything changes.

17
00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:55.657
A broken card gets replaced.

18
00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:59.298
Tomorrow, 10.10.1.20 goes to a different laptop.

19
00:00:59.298 --> 00:01:07.185
And Docker, which runs these little lab computers, gives each one a new random MAC every time it starts.

20
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.509
Nobody could keep that file right.

21
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.942
So let's borrow a trick from a café at lunchtime.

22
00:01:12.942 --> 00:01:16.981
The barista calls out, "Flat white for Sam!" Everyone hears it.

23
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.933
One person stands up and gets the cup.

24
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:22.000
Next time, she'll remember Sam.

25
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:26.855
Notice what she doesn't do: keep a photo album of every customer's face.

26
00:01:26.855 --> 00:01:29.085
It would be out of date by Tuesday.

27
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:30.345
Let's map it.

28
00:01:30.345 --> 00:01:32.000
The barista is client.

29
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.213
Sam's name is the IP address, and Sam's face is the MAC.

30
00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:42.044
Calling to the whole room is a broadcast: a frame to an address that every card accepts.

31
00:01:42.044 --> 00:01:44.020
And remembering Sam is a cache.

32
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.953
So client asks.

33
00:01:45.953 --> 00:01:51.133
It broadcasts one question: who has 10.10.1.20?

34
00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:52.896
Tell 10.10.1.10.

35
00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:55.914
Inside, the target's MAC is all zeros.

36
00:01:55.914 --> 00:01:58.300
That empty field is the question.

37
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:00.963
Who will hear it, and who will answer?

38
00:02:00.963 --> 00:02:01.972
Pause and guess.

39
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.120
The switch sends a broadcast out of every port, so everyone hears it.

40
00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:15.752
Router isn't 10.10.1.20, and it has never met client, so it drops the question.

41
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.005
Peer is 10.10.1.20.

42
00:02:18.005 --> 00:02:24.653
It writes client down, and answers: 10.10.1.20 is at this card.

43
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.272
The answer goes to client's card alone.

44
00:02:27.272 --> 00:02:32.258
The switch learned client's port from the question, so router never even hears it.

45
00:02:32.258 --> 00:02:34.691
Only the question needed the whole room.

46
00:02:34.967 --> 00:02:38.381
This is ARP, the Address Resolution Protocol.

47
00:02:38.381 --> 00:02:43.769
It turns an IP address into a MAC, and it has done that job since 1982.

48
00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:48.529
Client keeps the answer in its ARP cache, a short list of recent answers.

49
00:02:48.529 --> 00:02:51.855
Now the ping leaves at once, addressed to peer's card.

50
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.866
And peer answers without asking anything back.

51
00:02:54.866 --> 00:02:57.212
It wrote client down from the question.

52
00:02:57.467 --> 00:03:03.084
Two ARP messages, and from now on, every frame between them goes to one card only.

53
00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:05.419
Here the café picture breaks.

54
00:03:05.419 --> 00:03:09.381
A barista might notice a stranger grabbing Sam's coffee.

55
00:03:09.381 --> 00:03:10.725
ARP checks nothing.

56
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.956
Any card on the link can answer for any IP address, and the asker believes it.

57
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.163
ARP trusts everyone: lovely in a friend, worrying in a network.

58
00:03:20.163 --> 00:03:24.000
The lesson's Go deeper shows what an attacker does with that.

59
00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:25.363
Two more limits.

60
00:03:25.363 --> 00:03:27.144
ARP reaches one link only.

61
00:03:27.144 --> 00:03:32.351
For a computer on another network, client asks for the router's MAC instead.

62
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.507
And a remembered answer can go out of date, so entries are forgotten on purpose.

63
00:03:37.507 --> 00:03:39.163
The next section shows how.

64
00:03:39.433 --> 00:03:41.701
So where did client get peer's MAC?

65
00:03:41.701 --> 00:03:43.256
It asked the room, once.

66
00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:46.301
Ask everyone, answer one, remember for a while.

67
00:03:46.733 --> 00:03:47.834
One quick check.

68
00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:49.898
Now peer wants to ping client.

69
00:03:49.898 --> 00:03:52.443
Does peer have to ask the room first?

70
00:03:52.443 --> 00:03:53.544
Pause and think.

71
00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:57.014
No.

72
00:03:57.014 --> 00:03:59.937
Peer wrote client down from the question.

73
00:03:59.937 --> 00:04:01.577
It sends straight away.
